h respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]\n$$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$"] [:db/cas 17592186529118 :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{10}{\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{2n}.$" "Evaluate the geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{10}{\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{2n}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529119 :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-7)(0.3)^{2n}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-7)(0.3)^{2n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186529120 :question/problem "$\\,$ $x\\le \\frac{1}{3}\\text{ and }y>\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" "$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle x\\le \\frac{1}{3}\\text{ and }y>\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}"] [:db/cas 17592186529120 :question/worked-solution "De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$x\\le \\frac{1}{3}\\text{ and }y>\\frac{1}{2}$\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(x\\le \\frac{1}{3}\\text{ and }y>\\frac{1}{2}) & ≡¬(x\\le \\frac{1}{3}∧y>\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & ≡¬(x\\le \\frac{1}{3})∨¬(y>\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & ≡x>\\frac{1}{3}∨y\\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ≡x>\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ or }y\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$$\n\\displaystyle x\\le \\frac{1}{3}\\text{ and }y>\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(x\\le \\frac{1}{3}\\text{ and }y>\\frac{1}{2}) & ≡¬(x\\le \\frac{1}{3}∧y>\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & ≡¬(x\\le \\frac{1}{3})∨¬(y>\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & ≡x>\\frac{1}{3}∨y\\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ≡x>\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ or }y\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529124 :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186529124 :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $4.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{4},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{4}=\\frac{16}{81} \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{5}=\\frac{32}{243}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{4}}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{32}{243}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{16}{81}\\right)}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{16}{81}\\right)}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{16}{81}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $4.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum a{r}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{4},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{4}=\\frac{16}{81} \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{5}=\\frac{32}{243}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{4}}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{32}{243}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{16}{81}\\right)}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{16}{81}\\right)}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{16}{81}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529125 :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186529125 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=8{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{1}=3.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{8{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{2}}{8{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(1-\\frac{3}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum a{r}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=8{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{1}=3.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{8{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{2}}{8{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(1-\\frac{3}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529129 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212 \\\\ 0.866 & -0.173 \\\\ -0.866 & -1.212 \\\\ -0.866 & 0.173\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.670 & 0.224 & 0.224 & -0.671 \\\\ 0.224 & -0.671 & 0.671 & 0.224 \\\\ -0.670 & -0.224 & 0.224 & -0.671 \\\\ -0.224 & 0.671 & 0.671 & 0.224\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.191 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.095 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212 \\\\ 0.866 & -0.173 \\\\ -0.866 & -1.212 \\\\ -0.866 & 0.173\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$" "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212 \\\\ 0.866 & -0.173 \\\\ -0.866 & -1.212 \\\\ -0.866 & 0.173\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.670 & 0.224 & 0.224 & -0.671 \\\\ 0.224 & -0.671 & 0.671 & 0.224 \\\\ -0.670 & -0.224 & 0.224 & -0.671 \\\\ -0.224 & 0.671 & 0.671 & 0.224\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.191 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.095 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212 \\\\ 0.866 & -0.173 \\\\ -0.866 & -1.212 \\\\ -0.866 & 0.173\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529130 :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186529130 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=4{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{4{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2}}{4{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum a{r}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=4{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{4{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2}}{4{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529134 :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n\nII. $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n\nIII. $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n\nII. $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n\nIII. $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).\n$$*"] [:db/cas 17592186529136 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1.26 & 0.32 \\\\ 0.06 & -1.27 \\\\ 1.19 & -0.18 \\\\ 0.02 & 1.12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.583 & -0.434 & 0.687 & 0.016 \\\\ 0.49 & -0.561 & 0.046 & 0.666 \\\\ 0.506 & 0.466 & 0.725 & -0.029 \\\\ -0.405 & 0.529 & -0.027 & 0.746\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}1.917 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.528 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.709 & 0.706 \\\\ -0.706 & 0.709\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}-0.583 \\\\ 0.49 \\\\ 0.506 \\\\ -0.405\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 1.917$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}-1.26 & 0.32 \\\\ 0.06 & -1.27 \\\\ 1.19 & -0.18 \\\\ 0.02 & 1.12\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{aligned}]$" "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1.26 & 0.32 \\\\ 0.06 & -1.27 \\\\ 1.19 & -0.18 \\\\ 0.02 & 1.12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.583 & -0.434 & 0.687 & 0.016 \\\\ 0.49 & -0.561 & 0.046 & 0.666 \\\\ 0.506 & 0.466 & 0.725 & -0.029 \\\\ -0.405 & 0.529 & -0.027 & 0.746\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}1.917 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.528 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.709 & 0.706 \\\\ -0.706 & 0.709\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}-0.583 \\\\ 0.49 \\\\ 0.506 \\\\ -0.405\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 1.917\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}-1.26 & 0.32 \\\\ 0.06 & -1.27 \\\\ 1.19 & -0.18 \\\\ 0.02 & 1.12\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529137 :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$ under this inner product."] [:db/cas 17592186529139 :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{3} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{rank}(A)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=3$,  it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=3$ which, in turn, means that  $A$ has $3$ pivot columns, not $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Since $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{3} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{rank}(A)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=3$,  it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=3$ which, in turn, means that  $A$ has $3$ pivot columns, not $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186529140 :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{n}.$" "Evaluate the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{n}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529142 :