] [:db/cas 17592186529160 :question/problem "Find the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186529175 :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529180 :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }180{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{n}.$" "Evaluate the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }180{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{n}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529182 :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529191 :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately. \n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Null}(A)$ has no basis.* $\\,\\times$ \n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A)\\ne 0,$ we have $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}$ which, in turn, means that $\\text{Null}(A)$ must have a basis.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is **not** invertible.* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, by the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A)\\ne 0,$ we must have that $A$ is **not** invertible.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.* $\\,\\times$\n\nSince $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{n},$ which means that not all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately. \n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Null}(A)$ has no basis.* $\\,\\times$ \n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A)\\ne 0,$ we have $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}$ which, in turn, means that $\\text{Null}(A)$ must have a basis.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is **not** invertible.* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, by the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A)\\ne 0,$ we must have that $A$ is **not** invertible.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.* $\\,\\times$\n\nSince $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{n},$ which means that not all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186529194 :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{n}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{n}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530418 :question/problem "Given the function \n$f(x;w)={w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2},$\ncompute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$ with the input $x={[\\begin{aligned}5,\\,3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{w}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Given the function \n$f(x;w)={w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2},$\ncompute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$ with the input $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}5,\\,3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{w}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186530418 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $w.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2})={x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2})={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, with $x={[\\begin{aligned}5,\\,3\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $w.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2})={x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2})={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, with $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}5,\\,3\\end{aligned}]}^{T},\n$$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530419 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530421 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}.$ What is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}.$ What is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530421 :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a scalar-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the row vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3},$ and\n\n- outputs a scalar $f(x)\\in R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} & {x}_{1}+{x}_{3} & 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times 3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the total derivative at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}({x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}0+3(2) & 1+2 & 3(1)+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The total derivative of a scalar-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the row vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3},$ and\n\n- outputs a scalar $f(x)\\in R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} & {x}_{1}+{x}_{3} & 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times 3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the total derivative at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}({x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}0+3(2) & 1+2 & 3(1)+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530430 :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a scalar-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the row vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(x)$ where $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} & {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} & 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The total derivative of a scalar-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the row vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(x)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} & {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} & 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530434 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=2x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=2x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/33/330e2a1dd9f7d2cbcadb61fe49e767d95ee76a2cb669a9917ef3f6333719b98f.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=2x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=2x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/33/330e2a1dd9f7d2cbcadb61fe49e767d95ee76a2cb669a9917ef3f6333719b98f.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186530436 :question/problem "![](images/51/51ffadb1da41e6a1fe06fdc9c14a6967d3a80b4d26c6a830a401105e0f18bbf5.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,2]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}.\n\nFinally, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector {{field-9}} as they move towards the origin, the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue) is \n{{field-10}}.\n\nTherefore,\n{{field-11}} \n$<0.$" "![](images/51/51ffadb1da41e6a1fe06fdc9c14a6967d3a80b4d26c6a830a401105e0f18bbf5.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,2]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}.\n\nFinally, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector {{field-9}} as they move towards the origin, the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue) is \n{{field-10}}.\n\nTherefore,\n{{field-11}} \n$<0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186530436 :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny=-2x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/6e/6ea2d64a390187a28cfcd1c2692e97e5dc2ca5cb37649ba12cf71864e36958ea.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny=2x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2f2340cbff80765a5d3b551e87eb4207eebad05970e366ceeccd7bb142c0b677.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are negative. The eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue) is the one closest to $0.$\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{1}$ as they move towards the origin, the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior is ${\\lambda }_{1}.$\n\nTherefore, ${\\lambda }_{2}<{\\lambda }_{1}<0.$" "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny=-2x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/6e/6ea2d64a390187a28cfcd1c2692e97e5dc2ca5cb37649ba12cf71864e36958ea.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny=2x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2f2340cbff80765a5d3b551e87eb4207eebad05970e366ceeccd7bb142c0b677.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are negative. The eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue) is the one closest to $0.$\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{1}$ as they move towards the origin, the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior is ${\\lambda }_{1}.$\n\nTherefore, ${\\lambda }_{2}<{\\lambda }_{1}<0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186530452 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2+2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}-1{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ -{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ -{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2+2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}-1{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $$\n\\displaystyle x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ -{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ -{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530453 :question/problem "Express $3.\\overset{―}{03}$ as a geometric series.\n\n$3.\\overset{―}{03}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}$($\n{{field-2}}$)^{n}$" "Express $3.\\overset{―}{03}$ as a geometric series.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 3.\\overset{―}{03}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\n$$\n{{field-1}}$($\n{{field-2}}$)^{n}$"] [:db/cas 17592186530454 :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\sqrt[4]{6}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{4})=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}(1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\cdot 2|t|\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t⟹{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{3}{16}\\text{d}u.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt[4]{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{3}{16}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[(9)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(27-1) \\\\  & =13\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\sqrt[4]{6}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{4})=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}(1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\cdot 2|t|\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}.$ Then, $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{d}u=\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t⟹{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{3}{16}\\text{d}u.\n$$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt[4]{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{3}{16}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[(9)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(27-1) \\\\  & =13\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530462 :question/problem "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x-6)^{n}$ converges at  $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\nI. The series converges at $x=2$\n\nII. The series converges at $x=4$\n\nIII. The series diverges at $x=\\frac{7}{2}$" "Given that the power series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x-6)^{n}\n$$ converges at  $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\nI. The series converges at $x=2$\n\nII. The series converges at $x=4$\n\nIII. The series diverges at $x=\\frac{7}{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186530462 :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=6,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $\\frac{7}{2},$ i.e., $R\\ge \\frac{7}{2}.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}).$\n\n![](images/f7/f7b2a20d097d3ff34f92ff7f0015c449150575420dd53132d0b2703567628ef5.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $2\\notin [\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $4\\in [\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}),$ the series is convergent at $x=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\frac{7}{2}\\in [\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}),$ the series is convergent at $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "The center of the expansion is $x=6,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $\\frac{7}{2},$ i.e., $R\\ge \\frac{7}{2}.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}).$\n\n![](images/f7/f7b2a20d097d3ff34f92ff7f0015c449150575420dd53132d0b2703567628ef5.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $2\\notin [\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $4\\in [\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}),$ the series is convergent at $x=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{2}\\in [\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}),\n$$ the series is convergent at $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186530468 :question/problem "Given the function \n$f(x;w)={w}_{1}{x}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3}^{2},$\ncompute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$ with the input $x={[\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,-2,\\,-3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{w}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Given the function \n$f(x;w)={w}_{1}{x}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3}^{2},$\ncompute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$ with the input $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,-2,\\,-3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{w}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186530468 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $w.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3}^{2})={x}_{1}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3}^{2})={x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{3}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{3}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3}^{2})={x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, with $x={[\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,-2,\\,-3\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $w.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3}^{2})={x}_{1}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3}^{2})={x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{3}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{3}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3}^{2})={x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, with $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,-2,\\,-3\\end{aligned}]}^{T},\n$$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530470 :question/problem "Express $0.4\\overset{―}{25}$ using a geometric series.\n\n$0.4\\overset{―}{25}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-2}}$(${{field-3}}$)^{n}$" "Express $0.4\\overset{―}{25}$ using a geometric series.\n\n$0.4\\overset{―}{25}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\n$$\n{{field-2}}$(${{field-3}}$)^{n}$"] [:db/cas 17592186530472 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530482 :question/problem "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x-4)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-1,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\nI. The series diverges at $x=3$\n\nII. The series converges at $x=-2$\n\nIII. The series converges at $x=1$" "Given that the power series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x-4)^{n}\n$$ converges at  $x=-1,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\nI. The series diverges at $x=3$\n\nII. The series converges at $x=-2$\n\nIII. The series converges at $x=1$"] [:db/cas 17592186530487 :question/problem "Express $0.15\\overset{―}{3}$ using a geometric series.\n\n$0.15\\overset{―}{3}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-2}}$(${{field-3}}$)^{n}$" "Express $0.15\\overset{―}{3}$ using a geometric series.\n\n$0.15\\overset{―}{3}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\n$$\n{{field-2}}$(${{field-3}}$)^{n}$"] [:db/cas 17592186530490 :question/problem "Given the function \n$f(x;w)={w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}-{w}_{3}{x}_{3},$\ncompute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$ with the input $x={[\\begin{aligned}3,\\,2,\\,1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{w}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Given the function \n$f(x;w)={w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}-{w}_{3}{x}_{3},$\ncompute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$ with the input $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}3,\\,2,\\,1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{w}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186530490 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $w.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}-{w}_{3}{x}_{3})={x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}-{w}_{3}{x}_{3})={x}_{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{3}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{3}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}-{w}_{3}{x}_{3})=-{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, with $x={[\\begin{aligned}3,\\,2,\\,1\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $w.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}-{w}_{3}{x}_{3})={x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}-{w}_{3}{x}_{3})={x}_{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{3}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{3}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}-{w}_{3}{x}_{3})=-{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, with $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}3,\\,2,\\,1\\end{aligned}]}^{T},\n$$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530491 :question/problem "Express $6.\\overset{―}{3}$ using a geometric series.\n\n$6.\\overset{―}{3}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-2}}$(${{field-3}}$)^{n}$" "Express $6.\\overset{―}{3}$ using a geometric series.\n\n$6.\\overset{―}{3}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\n$$\n{{field-2}}$(${{field-3}}$)^{n}$"] [:db/cas 17592186530500 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5-5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5-5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}. \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2+t){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5-5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5-5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $$\n\\displaystyle x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}. \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2+t){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530507 :question/problem "Express $6.\\overset{―}{6}$ as a geometric series.\n\n$6.\\overset{―}{6}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}$($\n{{field-2}}$)^{n}$" "Express $6.\\overset{―}{6}$ as a geometric series.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 6.\\overset{―}{6}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\n$$\n{{field-1}}$($\n{{field-2}}$)^{n}$"] [:db/cas 17592186530517 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4-4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4-4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{5},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{5}.$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4-4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4-4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{5},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186530525 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530527 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/e5/e5c236defa66a00f12cd338ed0012cf91e4d7c15aa4865fc2b1edf5b0e2ea1da.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/e5/e5c236defa66a00f12cd338ed0012cf91e4d7c15aa4865fc2b1edf5b0e2ea1da.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186530529 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2+3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4+3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}+{w}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -{w}_{1}-{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={e}^{-3t}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2+3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4+3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}+{w}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -{w}_{1}-{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $$\n\\displaystyle x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={e}^{-3t}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530536 :question/problem "Given the function \n$f(x;w)={w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3},$\ncompute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$ with the input $x={[\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,-2,\\,4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{w}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Given the function \n$f(x;w)={w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3},$\ncompute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$ with the input $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,-2,\\,4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{w}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186530536 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $w.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3})={x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3})=-{x}_{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{3}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{3}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3})={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, with $x={[\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,-2,\\,4\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $w.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3})={x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3})=-{x}_{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{3}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{3}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3})={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, with $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,-2,\\,4\\end{aligned}]}^{T},\n$$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530652 :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b693165c8f6201e96f0a972746324c65a308d1f143f229c465b6aa22b7aeb16.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}5\\left((x-y)\\right)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $16{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=400,$ traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(5,0),$ as shown above?\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" "![](images/1b/1b693165c8f6201e96f0a972746324c65a308d1f143f229c465b6aa22b7aeb16.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}5\\left((x-y)\\right)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $16{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=400,$ traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(5,0),$ as shown above?\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186530747 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,2 t + 1\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- x\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,2 t + 1\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- x\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*"] [:db/cas 17592186530752 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t + 1,t + 1\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(x\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t + 1,t + 1\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(x\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*"] [:db/cas 17592186530755 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,t\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,t\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*"] [:db/cas 17592186530765 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left(y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y=x^{2} + 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi].$" "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left(y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve $y=x^{2} + 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi].$"] [:db/cas 17592186530767 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t - 2,t - 2\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(3 x\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t - 2,t - 2\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(3 x\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*"] [:db/cas 17592186530769 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,2 t + 1\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(3 x\\right)\\,i+\\left(y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,2 t + 1\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(3 x\\right)\\,i+\\left(y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*"] [:db/cas 17592186531566 :question/problem "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$" "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531566 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531572 :question/problem "$\\frac{11p{q}^{2}}{{r}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{5{r}^{2}}{121{p}^{4}q}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{11p{q}^{2}}{{r}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{5{r}^{2}}{121{p}^{4}q}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531577 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n$$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186531577 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{2}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 0 & 2{y}_{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\cdot 1 \\\\ -1-1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & {x}_{1} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{2}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 0 & 2{y}_{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\cdot 1 \\\\ -1-1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & {x}_{1} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531580 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{4{z}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{8xz}{y}?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5{y}^{2}}{4{z}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{8xz}{y}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531590 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=1\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-4)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}].$" "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=1\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-4)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531594 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=(-2)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 5+{e}^{5t}\\cdot 0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+1\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 0+{e}^{5t}\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}].$" "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=(-2)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 5+{e}^{5t}\\cdot 0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+1\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 0+{e}^{5t}\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531596 :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation." "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation."] [:db/cas 17592186531598 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}+4{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+8{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}+4{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+8{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n$$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186531598 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}(2{y}_{1}+4{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}(2{y}_{1}+4{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+8{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+8{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\ {1}^{2}-{0}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} & 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & -2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}(2{y}_{1}+4{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}(2{y}_{1}+4{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+8{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+8{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\ {1}^{2}-{0}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} & 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & -2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531611 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}{y}_{2}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}{y}_{2}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n$$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186531611 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}{y}_{2}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}{y}_{2}^{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 1 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{3} & 3{y}_{1}^{2}{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot (-1) \\\\ -{1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(-{x}_{1}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(-{x}_{1}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & {x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1} & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}{y}_{2}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}{y}_{2}^{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 1 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{3} & 3{y}_{1}^{2}{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot (-1) \\\\ -{1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(-{x}_{1}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(-{x}_{1}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & {x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1} & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531614 :question/problem "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}-{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$" "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}-{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531614 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & 30 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & 30 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531620 :question/problem "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}-{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]?$" "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}-{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531620 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-19 & 25 \\\\ 9 & -11\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}19 & -88 \\\\ -9 & 40\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-19 & 25 \\\\ 9 & -11\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}19 & -88 \\\\ -9 & 40\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531621 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-{x}_{2}^{2}\\le {x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So,  in turn, $Q(x)\\le 1.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $1.$ But before we conclude that $1$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=1.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(1)^{2}-(0)^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $1.$" "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-{x}_{2}^{2}\\le {x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So,  in turn, $Q(x)\\le 1.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $1.$ But before we conclude that $1$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=1.$\n\nNotice that when $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(1)^{2}-(0)^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186531628 :question/problem "![](images/83/83813bff1ff86036e2773d9476150de619688daec9cae2623a555a43290cace3.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" "![](images/83/83813bff1ff86036e2773d9476150de619688daec9cae2623a555a43290cace3.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186531633 :question/problem "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$" "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531633 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ {y}_{2} & {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ {y}_{2} & {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531636 :question/problem "$\\frac{4{s}^{2}}{t}\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{4{s}^{2}}{t}\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531637 :question/problem "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}-3{y}_{2} \\\\ 4{y}_{1}+6{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$" "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}-3{y}_{2} \\\\ 4{y}_{1}+6{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531637 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 21 \\\\ -2 & -24\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 & 63 \\\\ -82 & -102\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 21 \\\\ -2 & -24\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 & 63 \\\\ -82 & -102\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531645 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}-3{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}-3{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n$$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186531645 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1} & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}2(1) \\\\ 1-3(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(2{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(2{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}-3{x}_{1}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}-3{x}_{1})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1} & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}2(1) \\\\ 1-3(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(2{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(2{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}-3{x}_{1}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}-3{x}_{1})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531650 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{{x}^{2}-16}{{x}^{2}-5x+6}\\cdot \\frac{x-3}{x-4}?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}-16}{{x}^{2}-5x+6}\\cdot \\frac{x-3}{x-4}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531652 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=(-3)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+7\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-1)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(-2)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}].$" "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=(-3)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+7\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-1)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(-2)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531654 :question/problem "$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2y}\\cdot \\frac{6{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2y}\\cdot \\frac{6{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531660 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n$$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186531660 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}(2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}(2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{2} & 2{y}_{1} \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2} & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}2(1) \\\\ 3(-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(2{x}_{1}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(2{x}_{1}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(3{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(3{x}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & -24 \\\\ 24 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}(2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}(2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{2} & 2{y}_{1} \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2} & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}2(1) \\\\ 3(-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(2{x}_{1}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(2{x}_{1}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(3{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(3{x}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & -24 \\\\ 24 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531861 :question/problem "What is the range of the function $y=- \\frac{7}{3}\\sin(x+90^{\\circ})+\\frac{15}{4}$?" "What is the range of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=- \\frac{7}{3}\\sin(x+90^{\\circ})+\\frac{15}{4}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186531882 :question/problem "What is the range of the function $y=- \\frac{9}{2}\\sin(3x+\\pi)-\\frac{10}{3}$?" "What is the range of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=- \\frac{9}{2}\\sin(3x+\\pi)-\\frac{10}{3}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186531895 :question/problem "What is the range of the function $y=- \\frac{5}{2}\\cos(x-15^{\\circ})-\\frac{14}{3}$?" "What is the range of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=- \\frac{5}{2}\\cos(x-15^{\\circ})-\\frac{14}{3}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186531909 :question/problem "What is the range of the function $y=- \\frac{4}{3}\\sin(4x+\\frac{\\pi}{6})+\\frac{14}{3}$?" "What is the range of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=- \\frac{4}{3}\\sin(4x+\\frac{\\pi}{6})+\\frac{14}{3}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186531915 :question/problem "What is the minimum value of $f(x)=- \\frac{5}{2}\\cos(5x-2)-\\frac{11}{2}$?" "What is the minimum value of $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=- \\frac{5}{2}\\cos(5x-2)-\\frac{11}{2}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186532621 :question/problem "Starting with the standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x),$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion for $\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Starting with the standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x),$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion for $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532622 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{4}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{4}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532622 :question/worked-solution "Writing $b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={b}^{T}x.\n$$\n\nThe gradient of a function $f(x)={b}^{T}x$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=b.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={b}^{T}x.\n$$\n\nThe gradient of a function $f(x)={b}^{T}x$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=b.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532629 :question/problem "Using the standard form of the Maclaurin series of $\\frac{1}{1+x},$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion of the definite integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Using the standard form of the Maclaurin series of $\\frac{1}{1+x},$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion of the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532632 :question/problem "Using the standard form of the Maclaurin series of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion of the definite integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{x})\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Using the standard form of the Maclaurin series of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion of the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{x})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532638 :question/problem "Starting with the standard Maclaurin expansion for ${e}^{x},$ find the first four non-zero terms of the series expansion for $\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}-1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Starting with the standard Maclaurin expansion for ${e}^{x},$ find the first four non-zero terms of the series expansion for $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{e}^{x}-1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532640 :question/problem "Express $S=\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{3}{14}+\\frac{4}{21}+⋯+\\frac{21}{140}+\\frac{22}{147}$ in sigma notation.\n\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,\\,\\,\\,S=$ | $\\sum $ | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{}^{n=1}$ |  |  | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$ |" "Express $$\n\\displaystyle S=\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{3}{14}+\\frac{4}{21}+⋯+\\frac{21}{140}+\\frac{22}{147}\n$$ in sigma notation.\n\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,\\,\\,\\,S=$ | $\\sum $ | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{}^{n=1}$ |  |  | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186532643 :question/problem "Given that $u=x+3$, which integral is equivalent to $\\int x\\sqrt[4]{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Given that $u=x+3$, which integral is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\sqrt[4]{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532645 :question/problem "Find the sum $\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }-1-\\frac{(\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }-1)^{3}}{3}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }-1)^{n}}{n}+⋯.$" "Find the sum $$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt[3]{\\pi }-1-\\frac{(\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }-1)^{3}}{3}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }-1)^{n}}{n}+⋯.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532646 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-2b)^{T}(Ax-2b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-2b)^{T}(Ax-2b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532646 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-2b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-2b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-2b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-2b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-2b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-2b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532648 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(2Ax+b)^{T}(2Ax+b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(2Ax+b)^{T}(2Ax+b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532648 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=2Ax+b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2Ax+b)=2A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & =(2A)^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & =(2A)^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =4\\cdot {A}^{T}(2Ax+b) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}(2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}](2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-24 \\\\ -68\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=2Ax+b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2Ax+b)=2A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & =(2A)^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & =(2A)^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =4\\cdot {A}^{T}(2Ax+b) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}(2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}](2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-24 \\\\ -68\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532650 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532650 :question/worked-solution "Writing $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532651 :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $1-\\frac{e}{4}+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{16}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{e}^{n}}{{4}^{n}}+⋯\\,?$" "What is the sum of the series $$\n\\displaystyle 1-\\frac{e}{4}+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{16}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{e}^{n}}{{4}^{n}}+⋯\\,?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532652 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186532652 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+1+1 \\\\ 1+2-2 \\\\ 1-2+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+1+1 \\\\ 1+2-2 \\\\ 1-2+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532659 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}{-(n+1)}^{2}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}{-(n+1)}^{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532660 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186532660 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4-3 \\\\ 3+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4-3 \\\\ 3+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532661 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532661 :question/worked-solution "Writing $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532664 :question/problem "Using the standard form of the Maclaurin series of ${e}^{x},$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion of the definite integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1-{e}^{-x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Using the standard form of the Maclaurin series of ${e}^{x},$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion of the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1-{e}^{-x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532665 :question/problem "Find the sum $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}}{2!}+⋯+\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{n}}{n!}+⋯.$" "Find the sum $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}}{2!}+⋯+\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{n}}{n!}+⋯.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532668 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{4}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{4}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532668 :question/worked-solution "Writing $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={b}^{T}x.\n$$\n\nThe gradient of a function $f(x)={b}^{T}x$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=b.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={b}^{T}x.\n$$\n\nThe gradient of a function $f(x)={b}^{T}x$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=b.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532671 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532671 :question/worked-solution "Writing $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532674 :question/problem "Find the sum of the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$1-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{162}+⋯+\\frac{(-\\pi )^{n}}{{3}^{n}n!}+⋯=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find the sum of the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 1-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{162}+⋯+\\frac{(-\\pi )^{n}}{{3}^{n}n!}+⋯=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186532679 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $2\\pm 3\\text{i}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">$2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>" "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $2\\pm 3\\text{i}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">$2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186532683 :question/problem "Express ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(\\frac{n}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})$ as a sum of four terms." "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(\\frac{n}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})\n$$ as a sum of four terms."] [:db/cas 17592186532685 :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{50}+\\frac{1}{375}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n{5}^{n}}+⋯=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{50}+\\frac{1}{375}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n{5}^{n}}+⋯=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532686 :question/problem "Using the standard form of the Maclaurin series of $\\frac{1}{1-x},$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion of the definite integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Using the standard form of the Maclaurin series of $\\frac{1}{1-x},$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion of the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532687 :question/problem "Find the sum of the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$1+\\frac{5}{9}+\\frac{25}{81}+⋯+\\frac{{5}^{n}}{{9}^{n}}+⋯=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find the sum of the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 1+\\frac{5}{9}+\\frac{25}{81}+⋯+\\frac{{5}^{n}}{{9}^{n}}+⋯=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186532689 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186532689 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0+0 \\\\ 0+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0+0 \\\\ 0+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532691 :question/problem "Starting with the standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion for $\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Starting with the standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion for $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532692 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186532692 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-8 \\\\ -4+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-8 \\\\ -4+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532693 :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/df/dfc7359ad44caa959459676a1e54bf1575e3c7e0f771e92a74817d9d1f9cb581.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$" "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/df/dfc7359ad44caa959459676a1e54bf1575e3c7e0f771e92a74817d9d1f9cb581.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\n$$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532694 :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /4}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /4}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532695 :question/problem "Find the sum of the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$1-\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{4}{49}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{2}^{n}}{{7}^{n}}+⋯=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find the sum of the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 1-\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{4}{49}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{2}^{n}}{{7}^{n}}+⋯=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186532697 :question/problem "Find the sum $1-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{50}-\\frac{1}{750}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{5}^{n}n!}+⋯.$" "Find the sum $$\n\\displaystyle 1-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{50}-\\frac{1}{750}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{5}^{n}n!}+⋯.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532702 :question/problem "Find the sum of the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$1+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{{4}^{2}2!}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{{4}^{3}3!}+⋯+\\frac{{\\pi }^{n}}{{4}^{n}n!}+⋯=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find the sum of the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 1+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{{4}^{2}2!}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{{4}^{3}3!}+⋯+\\frac{{\\pi }^{n}}{{4}^{n}n!}+⋯=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186532704 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=2(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=2(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532704 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)=2{y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{T}y)=4{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 4y \\\\  & =4\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 16 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-68 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)=2{y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{T}y)=4{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 4y \\\\  & =4\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 16 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-68 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532708 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(5Ax-b)^{T}(5Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(5Ax-b)^{T}(5Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532708 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=5Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5Ax-b)=5A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & =(5A)^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & =(5A)^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =10\\cdot {A}^{T}(5Ax-b) \\\\  & =10\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}(5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =10\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}](5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =10\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =10\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =10\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 120 \\\\ 180\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=5Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5Ax-b)=5A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & =(5A)^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & =(5A)^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =10\\cdot {A}^{T}(5Ax-b) \\\\  & =10\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}(5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =10\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}](5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =10\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =10\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =10\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 120 \\\\ 180\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532715 :question/problem "![](images/c2/c27ab9b0062fabfe1fdbfd43631bf1f143c0fa4ec80587519e9ce6c4e84e7b2b.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above." "![](images/c2/c27ab9b0062fabfe1fdbfd43631bf1f143c0fa4ec80587519e9ce6c4e84e7b2b.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$ shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186532719 :question/problem "Find the sum $1-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{18}-\\frac{1}{162}+⋯+\\frac{(-1/3)^{n}}{n!}+⋯\\,.$" "Find the sum $$\n\\displaystyle 1-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{18}-\\frac{1}{162}+⋯+\\frac{(-1/3)^{n}}{n!}+⋯\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532721 :question/problem "$1+\\frac{1}{7}+\\frac{1}{49}+⋯+\\frac{1}{{7}^{n}}+⋯=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle 1+\\frac{1}{7}+\\frac{1}{49}+⋯+\\frac{1}{{7}^{n}}+⋯=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532723 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}x{(1+x)}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}x{(1+x)}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532724 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532724 :question/worked-solution "Writing $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532725 :question/problem "Starting with the standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion for $\\int \\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Starting with the standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion for $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532783 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=-6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{7}{8}\\left(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)\\right).$" "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{7}{8}\\left(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532783 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $B=\\left(-\\frac{7}{8}\\right)(4)=-\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{7}{2}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{7}{2}}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}.$" "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\left(-\\frac{7}{8}\\right)(4)=-\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{7}{2}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{7}{2}}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532784 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=-6\\cos\\left(-\\frac{7}{5}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$" "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-6\\cos\\left(-\\frac{7}{5}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532784 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{7}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{7}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{7}{5}}=\\frac{10\\pi }{7}.$" "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{7}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{7}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{7}{5}}=\\frac{10\\pi }{7}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532792 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=-4\\cos\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).$" "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-4\\cos\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532792 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{2}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{2}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{2}{5}}=5\\pi .$" "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{2}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{2}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{2}{5}}=5\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532798 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).$" "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532798 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{1}{4}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{1}{4}}=8\\pi .$" "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{1}{4}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{1}{4}}=8\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532803 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\right).$" "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532803 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $B=\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\right)(2)=-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{1}{3}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{1}{3}}=6\\pi .$" "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\right)(2)=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{1}{3}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{1}{3}}=6\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532817 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=2\\cos\\left(-\\frac{11}{10}\\left(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right).$" "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=2\\cos\\left(-\\frac{11}{10}\\left(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532817 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $B=\\left(-\\frac{11}{10}\\right)(4)=-\\frac{22}{5}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{22}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{22}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{22}{5}}=\\frac{5\\pi }{11}.$" "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\left(-\\frac{11}{10}\\right)(4)=-\\frac{22}{5}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{22}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{22}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{22}{5}}=\\frac{5\\pi }{11}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532819 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=-6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{9}{5}x+\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right).$" "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{9}{5}x+\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532819 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{9}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{9}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{9}{5}}=\\frac{10\\pi }{9}.$" "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{9}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{9}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{9}{5}}=\\frac{10\\pi }{9}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532825 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=-6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{11}{10}\\left(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right).$" "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{11}{10}\\left(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532825 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $B=\\left(-\\frac{11}{10}\\right)(5)=-\\frac{11}{2}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{11}{2}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{11}{2}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{11}{2}}=\\frac{4\\pi }{11}.$" "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\left(-\\frac{11}{10}\\right)(5)=-\\frac{11}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{11}{2}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{11}{2}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{11}{2}}=\\frac{4\\pi }{11}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532829 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|\\frac{1}{4}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{1}{4}}=8\\pi .$" "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|\\frac{1}{4}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{1}{4}}=8\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532831 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=-4\\cos\\left(-\\frac{1}{10}\\left(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\right).$" "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-4\\cos\\left(-\\frac{1}{10}\\left(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532831 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $B=\\left(-\\frac{1}{10}\\right)(4)=-\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{2}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{2}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{2}{5}}=5\\pi .$" "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\left(-\\frac{1}{10}\\right)(4)=-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{2}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{2}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{2}{5}}=5\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532843 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=-3\\cos\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).$" "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-3\\cos\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532843 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{1}{6}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{1}{6}}=12\\pi .$" "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{1}{6}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{1}{6}}=12\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533973 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.6+1.8+0.7+2.9+1.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{5}\\,{e}^{-7.5\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.6+1.8+0.7+2.9+1.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{5}\\,{e}^{-7.5\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533974 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=5.2,$ $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}=22.6,$ and $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-6/2}\\,{e}^{-(22.6-2(5.2)\\theta +6{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,-11.3\\,+\\,5.2\\theta \\,-\\,3{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-11.3+5.2\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=5.2,\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}=22.6,\n$$ and $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-6/2}\\,{e}^{-(22.6-2(5.2)\\theta +6{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,-11.3\\,+\\,5.2\\theta \\,-\\,3{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-11.3+5.2\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533976 :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n(n+1)}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n(n+1)}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533979 :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n(n+1)}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n(n+1)}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533981 :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{3n(n+1)}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{3n(n+1)}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533986 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=3({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=3({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533986 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}3{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=3{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=3(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=3([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5\\end{aligned}])=3([\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 15\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 30 \\\\ -3 & -15 \\\\ 9 & 45\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}3{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=3{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=3(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=3([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5\\end{aligned}])=3([\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 15\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 30 \\\\ -3 & -15 \\\\ 9 & 45\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533992 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=(det(X))^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $X=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=(det(X))^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $$\n\\displaystyle X=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533992 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $X=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 2}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=(det(X))^{2}=({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{22} & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{21}) \\\\ 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{12}) & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2det(X)[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $X=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=2(4\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}14 & -42 \\\\ 14 & 56\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle X=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 2},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=(det(X))^{2}=({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{22} & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{21}) \\\\ 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{12}) & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2det(X)[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle X=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=2(4\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}14 & -42 \\\\ 14 & 56\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533993 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X)).\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $X=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X)).\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $$\n\\displaystyle X=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533993 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $X=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 2}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X))=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}_{22}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{{x}_{21}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} \\\\ \\frac{{x}_{12}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{-{x}_{11}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $X=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{-4-(-3)} & \\frac{1}{-4-(-3)} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-4-(-3)} & -\\frac{(-1)}{-4-(-3)}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{-1} & \\frac{1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-1} & -\\frac{(-1)}{-1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle X=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 2},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X))=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}_{22}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{{x}_{21}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} \\\\ \\frac{{x}_{12}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{-{x}_{11}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle X=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{-4-(-3)} & \\frac{1}{-4-(-3)} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-4-(-3)} & -\\frac{(-1)}{-4-(-3)}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{-1} & \\frac{1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-1} & -\\frac{(-1)}{-1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533994 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=13$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 12\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=13$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 12\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533995 :question/problem "Evaluate the series\n${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(n+3)(n+4)}.$\nDo not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+4}.$*\n\n${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(n+3)(n+4)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the series\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(n+3)(n+4)}.\n$$\nDo not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+4}.\n$$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(n+3)(n+4)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186533998 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186533999 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534000 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534000 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534003 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{x}_{ij}^{2}\n$$\n\n \nwhere ${x}_{ij}$ are the corresponding matrix's entries. What is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $X=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{x}_{ij}^{2}\n$$\n\n \nwhere ${x}_{ij}$ are the corresponding matrix's entries. What is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $$\n\\displaystyle X=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534003 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $X=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 2}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(X)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{x}_{ij}^{2}=({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{11} & 2{x}_{12} \\\\ 2{x}_{21} & 2{x}_{22}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2X.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $X=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (1) & 2\\cdot (5) \\\\ 2\\cdot (-3) & 2\\cdot (4)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle X=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 2},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(X)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{x}_{ij}^{2}=({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{11} & 2{x}_{12} \\\\ 2{x}_{21} & 2{x}_{22}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2X.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle X=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (1) & 2\\cdot (5) \\\\ 2\\cdot (-3) & 2\\cdot (4)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534008 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=3({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=3({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534008 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}3{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=3{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=3(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=3([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}])=3([\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 14 \\\\ 10 & -15 & -35\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 18 & 42 \\\\ 30 & -45 & -105\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}3{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=3{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=3(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=3([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}])=3([\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 14 \\\\ 10 & -15 & -35\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 18 & 42 \\\\ 30 & -45 & -105\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534009 :question/problem "Evaluate the series\n${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}-6n+8}.$\nDo not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*\n\n${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}-6n+8}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the series\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}-6n+8}.\n$$\nDo not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}-6n+8}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186534013 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{-6t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/ff/ff337e0120343c7c956afa5e49ea3b482fbd79fe363783a018842d1bfeb340d6.png)" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-6t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/ff/ff337e0120343c7c956afa5e49ea3b482fbd79fe363783a018842d1bfeb340d6.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186534016 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{(n+3)(n+5)}.$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+5)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+5}).$*" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{(n+3)(n+5)}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+5)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+5}).\n$$*"] [:db/cas 17592186534018 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+11}{(x+6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+11}{(x+6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534022 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=\\frac{1}{2}(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=\\frac{1}{2}(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186534022 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{3}.$ Then, $L(A)=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y)=y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}={y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =([\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -70 \\\\ 0 & -35 \\\\ 0 & 63\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{3}.$ Then, $L(A)=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y)=y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}={y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =([\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -70 \\\\ 0 & -35 \\\\ 0 & 63\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534024 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{T-3}+\\frac{5}{T-4}-\\frac{5}{{T}^{2}-7T+12}=0.$" "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{T-3}+\\frac{5}{T-4}-\\frac{5}{{T}^{2}-7T+12}=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534027 :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=7}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n-3)(n-2)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=7}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n-3)(n-2)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186534028 :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{n(n+1)}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{n(n+1)}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534029 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534029 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-40 & 10 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-40 & 10 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534030 :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+2)(n+1)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+2)(n+1)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186534033 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{(n+2)(n+4)}.$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{1}{(n+2)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+4}).$*" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{(n+2)(n+4)}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{(n+2)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+4}).\n$$*"] [:db/cas 17592186534034 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534034 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}2{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=2(y{x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2([\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 7\\end{aligned}])=2([\\begin{aligned}-18 & 42 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-36 & 84 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 14\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}2{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=2(y{x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2([\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 7\\end{aligned}])=2([\\begin{aligned}-18 & 42 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-36 & 84 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 14\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534039 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8x-3}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{23}{25}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$" "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8x-3}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{23}{25}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$"] [:db/cas 17592186534045 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=9,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=9,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534046 :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}-7n+12}.$\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*\n\n${\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}-7n+12}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}-7n+12}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}-7n+12}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186534047 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=8,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=3,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=8,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=3,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534054 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=16$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=-13.2,$ $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}=33.2,$ and $n=16.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-16/2}\\,{e}^{-(33.2-2(-13.2)\\theta +16{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{256{\\pi }^{8}}\\,{e}^{\\,-16.6\\,-\\,13.2\\theta \\,-\\,8{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-16.6-13.2\\theta -8{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=16$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=-13.2,\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}=33.2,\n$$ and $n=16.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-16/2}\\,{e}^{-(33.2-2(-13.2)\\theta +16{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{256{\\pi }^{8}}\\,{e}^{\\,-16.6\\,-\\,13.2\\theta \\,-\\,8{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-16.6-13.2\\theta -8{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534057 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+4n+3}.$\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+4n+3}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*"] [:db/cas 17592186534065 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+x-1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+x-1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534070 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma }\\right)}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1.7+2.3+2.2+1.1+1.9+1.8 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(1.7)^{2}+(2.3)^{2}+(2.2)^{2}+(1.1)^{2}+(1.9)^{2}+(1.8)^{2} \\\\  & =21.08\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-6/2}\\,{e}^{-(21.08-2(11)\\theta +6{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,-10.54\\,+\\,11\\theta \\,-\\,3{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-10.54+11\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma }\\right)}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1.7+2.3+2.2+1.1+1.9+1.8 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(1.7)^{2}+(2.3)^{2}+(2.2)^{2}+(1.1)^{2}+(1.9)^{2}+(1.8)^{2} \\\\  & =21.08\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-6/2}\\,{e}^{-(21.08-2(11)\\theta +6{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,-10.54\\,+\\,11\\theta \\,-\\,3{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-10.54+11\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534071 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[1,\\text{ }-1]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/45/4522e4e6ed382e821aaa47d40a191135161f95eef276b6e8ba3a8514e6bfdfd8.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-7}} | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-8}} | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/db/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a6/a680b8c9319290df3beb20d7db964f385b368b01ead33961e2c10d22b19db4eb.png)" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[1,\\text{ }-1]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/45/4522e4e6ed382e821aaa47d40a191135161f95eef276b6e8ba3a8514e6bfdfd8.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-7}} | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-8}} | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/db/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a6/a680b8c9319290df3beb20d7db964f385b368b01ead33961e2c10d22b19db4eb.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186534071 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/45/4522e4e6ed382e821aaa47d40a191135161f95eef276b6e8ba3a8514e6bfdfd8.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-2t} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1.36$ | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | $4.08$ | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/db/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/13/13a57f14ea65118473c24c6eb1dbf50a0461060201ced051bb2eb6776f5daf64.png)" "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/45/4522e4e6ed382e821aaa47d40a191135161f95eef276b6e8ba3a8514e6bfdfd8.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-2t} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1.36$ | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | $4.08$ | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/db/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/13/13a57f14ea65118473c24c6eb1dbf50a0461060201ced051bb2eb6776f5daf64.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186534073 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$20$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$36$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$20$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$36$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186534073 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}..\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $2$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =2y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2([\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 20 \\\\ -18 & 36\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}..\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $2$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =2y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2([\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 20 \\\\ -18 & 36\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534074 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{5-3x}{{x}^{2}+2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{5-3x}{{x}^{2}+2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186534080 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">$0$</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">$-32$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">$0$</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">$-32$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186534080 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{3}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $2$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =2y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -32 & -32 \\\\ 16 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{3}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $2$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =2y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -32 & -32 \\\\ 16 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534082 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(2Ax-b)^{T}(2Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$-240$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2140$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(2Ax-b)^{T}(2Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$-240$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2140$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186534082 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=2Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=2Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}=2{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=2{x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot 2{x}_{j} \\\\  & =4{y}_{i}{x}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n The $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $4$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=4y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\n Finally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =4y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(2Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(2[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4([\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 110\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}48 \\\\ 428\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}480 & 480 & -240 \\\\ 4280 & 4280 & -2140\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=2Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=2Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}=2{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=2{x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot 2{x}_{j} \\\\  & =4{y}_{i}{x}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n The $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $4$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=4y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\n Finally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =4y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(2Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(2[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4([\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 110\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}48 \\\\ 428\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}480 & 480 & -240 \\\\ 4280 & 4280 & -2140\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534094 :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{n(n+1)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{n(n+1)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186534095 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}-3n+2}.$\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}-3n+2}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*"] [:db/cas 17592186534098 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}-4x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}-4x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186534178 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=\\langle 6 t + 4,- \\frac{\\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\frac{1}{3 t + 2}\\rangle$ from $t=0$ to $t=1$.\n\n![](images/db/db3e582e3cbf491015aa1f08ac77ddb6b02b2a8143bebc2d76d171ebd64f9570.png)" "Find the arc length of the curve $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=\\langle 6 t + 4,- \\frac{\\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\frac{1}{3 t + 2}\\rangle\n$$ from $t=0$ to $t=1$.\n\n![](images/db/db3e582e3cbf491015aa1f08ac77ddb6b02b2a8143bebc2d76d171ebd64f9570.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186534188 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=\\langle 4 t + 2,- \\frac{\\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\frac{1}{2 t + 1}\\rangle$ from $t=0$ to $t=1$.\n\n![](images/8d/8d83a591169c9ac41f6637a985554b0b907fa017349bf92c9197618a3f61409b.png)" "Find the arc length of the curve $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=\\langle 4 t + 2,- \\frac{\\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\frac{1}{2 t + 1}\\rangle\n$$ from $t=0$ to $t=1$.\n\n![](images/8d/8d83a591169c9ac41f6637a985554b0b907fa017349bf92c9197618a3f61409b.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186534217 :question/problem "If $R=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}],$ then $det(R)=${{field-1}}." "If $$\n\\displaystyle R=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then $det(R)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186534218 :question/problem "If $Q=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -7 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -5 & 6\\end{aligned}],$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}." "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -7 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -5 & 6\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186534226 :question/problem "If $H=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}],$ then $det(H)=${{field-1}}." "If $$\n\\displaystyle H=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then $det(H)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186534228 :question/problem "Find the value of $|\\begin{aligned}10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ -11 & 12 & -7 \\\\ 12 & 7 & 12\\end{aligned}|.$" "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ -11 & 12 & -7 \\\\ 12 & 7 & 12\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534229 :question/problem "Find the value of $|\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 5 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}|.$" "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 5 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534234 :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}],$ then $det(A)=${{field-1}}." "If $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then $det(A)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186534237 :question/problem "If $Q=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -10 & -5 & -12\\end{aligned}],$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}." "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -10 & -5 & -12\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186534244 :question/problem "If $Q=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 9 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & -9 & -10\\end{aligned}],$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}." "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 9 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & -9 & -10\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186534246 :question/problem "If $Q=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -12 & 5\\end{aligned}],$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}." "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -12 & 5\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186534251 :question/problem "If $W=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}],$ then $det(W)=${{field-1}}." "If $$\n\\displaystyle W=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then $det(W)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186534974 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}-\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{y}{x}\\,dy+dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}-\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{y}{x}\\,dy+dz,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$"] [:db/cas 17592186534979 :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?"] [:db/cas 17592186534979 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n}\\cdot n}{(9+5n)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{9+5n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\sqrt[n]{n}\\cdot \\frac{5n}{n})}{(\\frac{9}{n}+\\frac{5n}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[n]{n}}{(\\frac{9}{n}+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 1}{0+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=1,$ the root test gives no conclusion." "Suppose we have the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n}\\cdot n}{(9+5n)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{9+5n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\sqrt[n]{n}\\cdot \\frac{5n}{n})}{(\\frac{9}{n}+\\frac{5n}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[n]{n}}{(\\frac{9}{n}+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 1}{0+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=1,$ the root test gives no conclusion."] [:db/cas 17592186534981 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]?$"] [:db/cas 17592186534990 :question/worked-solution "![](images/1f/1ffb0a83abf7e736c47035f4f337c36a6bb129fd0d64fe0ac3bfe8e46c194a11.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" "![](images/1f/1ffb0a83abf7e736c47035f4f337c36a6bb129fd0d64fe0ac3bfe8e46c194a11.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },\n$$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534994 :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!}.$ Does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!}.$ Does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?"] [:db/cas 17592186534994 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{11}{7\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{n!}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{7}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{n!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{7}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{n!}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince $L=0<1,$ the series converges by the root test." "Suppose we have the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{11}{7\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{n!}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{7}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{n!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{7}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{n!}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince $L=0<1,$ the series converges by the root test."] [:db/cas 17592186534996 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ]?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ]?$"] [:db/cas 17592186534998 :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}.$ Does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}.$ Does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?"] [:db/cas 17592186534998 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\cdot {n}^{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{3}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{n}^{n-1/2}} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{3}}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=0<1,$ the series converges by the root test." "Suppose we have the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\cdot {n}^{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{3}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{n}^{n-1/2}} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{3}}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=0<1,$ the series converges by the root test."] [:db/cas 17592186535000 :question/problem "![](images/a5/a5f23af5dd514a65937eee29bb0992ff94d68897d8c6422fcf215aad45379c4a.png)\n\nConsider the part of parabola and constant vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the part of parabola is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" "![](images/a5/a5f23af5dd514a65937eee29bb0992ff94d68897d8c6422fcf215aad45379c4a.png)\n\nConsider the part of parabola and constant vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the part of parabola is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186535000 :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works with the particle for $y<0,$ while $F$  works against the particle for $y>0.$ \n\n![](images/53/537aca6190fc2f3e06e85c0781a8bb461559ef0a7515ee29aca505f9d615f820.png)\n\n \n\nBy symmetry, the contribution to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $y<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $y>0.$ So,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/49/4939f9a22400a7a56eaeacb77d520e522f50aa8378ca1014a19810122e12da51.png)\n\n \n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works with the particle for $y<0,$ while $F$  works against the particle for $y>0.$ \n\n![](images/53/537aca6190fc2f3e06e85c0781a8bb461559ef0a7515ee29aca505f9d615f820.png)\n\n \n\nBy symmetry, the contribution to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $y<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $y>0.$ So,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/49/4939f9a22400a7a56eaeacb77d520e522f50aa8378ca1014a19810122e12da51.png)\n\n \n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186535011 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(-2) \\\\ y(-2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"130px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(-2) \\\\ y(-2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"130px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186535011 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t+2)}\\,x(-2) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(t+2)) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(t+2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(t+2)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(t+2))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(t+2))\\, \\\\ \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(t+2))\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t+2)}\\,x(-2) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(t+2)) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(t+2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(t+2)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(t+2))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(t+2))\\, \\\\ \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(t+2))\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186535015 :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcf7a2ea5b44f3f5ba7b1663f7979cdc3fc8c1036b6cc797904ffda77f1003bd.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the сurve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" "![](images/dc/dcf7a2ea5b44f3f5ba7b1663f7979cdc3fc8c1036b6cc797904ffda77f1003bd.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the сurve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186535015 :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/81/818ad935fd7aa592f00c23c9c77799d127449b4bb6ffacee4354e7acf39887d8.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/70/70e268a08195a92573f41927ae7de653837fca273f0c75cb951243a31ec660a2.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/81/818ad935fd7aa592f00c23c9c77799d127449b4bb6ffacee4354e7acf39887d8.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/70/70e268a08195a92573f41927ae7de653837fca273f0c75cb951243a31ec660a2.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186535021 :question/worked-solution "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and collect the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100{x}^{2} & =7 \\\\ 100{x}^{2}-7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100{x}^{2}-7 & =0 \\\\ (10x)^{2}-{(\\sqrt{7})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (10x-\\sqrt{7})(10x+\\sqrt{7}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n10x-\\sqrt{7}=0\\,\\text{or}\\,10x+\\sqrt{7}=0.\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x-\\sqrt{7} & =0 & \\, & \\, & 10x+\\sqrt{7} & =0 \\\\ 10x & =\\sqrt{7} & \\, & \\, & 10x & =-\\sqrt{7} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10} & \\, & \\, & x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10},\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}.$ We can write this even more concisely as $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}.$" "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and collect the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100{x}^{2} & =7 \\\\ 100{x}^{2}-7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100{x}^{2}-7 & =0 \\\\ (10x)^{2}-{(\\sqrt{7})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (10x-\\sqrt{7})(10x+\\sqrt{7}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n10x-\\sqrt{7}=0\\,\\text{or}\\,10x+\\sqrt{7}=0.\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x-\\sqrt{7} & =0 & \\, & \\, & 10x+\\sqrt{7} & =0 \\\\ 10x & =\\sqrt{7} & \\, & \\, & 10x & =-\\sqrt{7} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10} & \\, & \\, & x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $$\n\\displaystyle x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10},\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}.\n$$ We can write this even more concisely as $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186535023 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,i+j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ]?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,i+j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ]?$"] [:db/cas 17592186535039 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4}{{z}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4}{{z}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186535053 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\pi ,$ since it is the only value that belongs to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\pi $ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=1 \\\\ f\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\pi )=1,$ so the global maximum is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=f\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\n![](images/7e/7e0008d35355afebc392a4bc508bd2cf2de5dc32a108f9f9c030d75a24ece076.png)" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\pi ,$ since it is the only value that belongs to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\pi $ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=1 \\\\ f\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\pi )=1,$ so the global maximum is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $$\n\\displaystyle f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=f\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,\n$$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\n![](images/7e/7e0008d35355afebc392a4bc508bd2cf2de5dc32a108f9f9c030d75a24ece076.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186535055 :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?"] [:db/cas 17592186535055 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{10n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{8n+1}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{10n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{n(8+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{10\\sqrt[n]{n}}{(8+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 1}{8+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=\\frac{5}{4}>1,$ the series diverges by the root test." "Suppose we have the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{10n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{8n+1}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{10n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{n(8+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{10\\sqrt[n]{n}}{(8+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 1}{8+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=\\frac{5}{4}>1,$ the series diverges by the root test."] [:db/cas 17592186535057 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy-z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix parametrized by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy-z\\,dz,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix parametrized by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$"] [:db/cas 17592186535064 :question/worked-solution "![](images/df/df5d54940a136a53c1e1c00f8cc9d71d7b064f6e0895a029da2738cf21998a56.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" "![](images/df/df5d54940a136a53c1e1c00f8cc9d71d7b064f6e0895a029da2738cf21998a56.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },\n$$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186535069 :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}.$ According to the root test, does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?"] [:db/cas 17592186535069 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{6}{5\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{{n}^{6}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[n]{n}}\\right)}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot {1}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=\\frac{6}{5}>1,$ the series diverges by the root test." "Suppose we have the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{6}{5\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{{n}^{6}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[n]{n}}\\right)}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot {1}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=\\frac{6}{5}>1,$ the series diverges by the root test."] [:db/cas 17592186535072 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+yz\\,dy+z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the line $r(t)=t\\,i+5t\\,j+\\,k$ for $t\\in [2,3]?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+yz\\,dy+z\\,dz,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the line $r(t)=t\\,i+5t\\,j+\\,k$ for $t\\in [2,3]?$"] [:db/cas 17592186535078 :question/problem "![](images/8a/8aed400e7768b70f4532642418d83b36d9e32aa84db87c71ea6d902504930bc4.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the semiellipse is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$" "![](images/8a/8aed400e7768b70f4532642418d83b36d9e32aa84db87c71ea6d902504930bc4.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the semiellipse is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186535078 :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/d8/d8e05de34bf9ddfdbf9580b1df32eb9e091f56f76ac3616cbb1cd81d5d212a86.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/17/17423916013677c1476df5a4368ab9eec81671b04b4ca29a0e702b20ddc818f2.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/d8/d8e05de34bf9ddfdbf9580b1df32eb9e091f56f76ac3616cbb1cd81d5d212a86.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/17/17423916013677c1476df5a4368ab9eec81671b04b4ca29a0e702b20ddc818f2.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186535079 :question/problem "![](images/3c/3c2035d50b823b9bcbeb3ae8cf698df9a25c95a289c5a6f35054269dc920088d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" "![](images/3c/3c2035d50b823b9bcbeb3ae8cf698df9a25c95a289c5a6f35054269dc920088d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186535079 :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/df/dfec76b47e8508a57e18440a72986bcae8e2bd19559bd0915ea4530e0b08b4f6.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/58/58e3efc39d0aee02d14e7c80c7355c37a1f28b6f0f54ff7102553e3a6ffc17cf.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/df/dfec76b47e8508a57e18440a72986bcae8e2bd19559bd0915ea4530e0b08b4f6.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/58/58e3efc39d0aee02d14e7c80c7355c37a1f28b6f0f54ff7102553e3a6ffc17cf.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186535087 :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{6t}z(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{6t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-6)t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $Z(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-6)t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t=Z(s-6),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-6>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+6.\n$$" "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{6t}z(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{6t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-6)t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $$\n\\displaystyle Z(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-6)t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t=Z(s-6),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-6>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+6.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186535088 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4}{3}{y}^{7}\\,\\text{d}y.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4}{3}{y}^{7}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186535305 :question/problem